Schema Architecture Documentation


Human-Centered Resource Directory Schema
What Gets Included in the Resource Listing
Plain-Language Standard
This documentation follows the principles of the federal Plain Writing Act of 2010 (Public Law 111-274). The Act directs covered federal agencies to use clear, concise, and well-organized communication that the public can understand and use. This project is not subject to the Act; the project follows the same plain-language principles as a professional standard.
The Core Structure of a Resource Listing
A resource listing is the information the website provides about a community resource and the services available from that resource.
The project organizes a resource listing into three basic parts:
The Resource — The resource itself, what it offers, and who it serves.
Operational Information — Where, when, and how a person can access the resource or service and what requirements apply.
Decision Information — Why the resource or service may be appropriate for a person's particular need.
The three parts allow the same resource information to be understood by people, Happi.mni, AI systems, and search engines.
What Is a Schema?
A schema is a standard way to describe and label information so computer systems can understand the information.
This website uses Schema.org, a shared vocabulary for describing information on websites.
For a resource listing, the schema identifies information such as:
What the resource is.
What the resource offers.
Who the resource is intended to help.
Where the resource or its services are available.
When the resource or its services are available.
How a person can access the resource or its services.
What requirements a person must meet.
Why the resource or its services may be a good match for a particular need.
1.1 The Resource
The Resource section identifies the resource itself, what it offers, and who it serves.
Information can include:
Resource name.
Description of the service.
Type of service.
Population served.
Services or specialties offered.
The Schema.org Service type identifies a service provided by an organization or other provider.
The Schema.org hasOfferCatalog property can identify several services or specialties offered by the same provider.
1.2 Operational Information
Operational Information explains where, when, and how a person can use the service and what requirements apply.
Information can include:
How can a person get the service? Online, in person, walk-in, telephone, or referral.
Who qualifies for the service? Eligibility requirements.
What does the service cost? Payment requirements or whether the service is free.
What insurance does the provider accept? Insurance accepted for the service.
Where can a person receive the service? Service location and address.
When can a person receive the service? Hours and availability.
What languages are available? Languages supported by the provider.
What kind of help does the provider offer? Medical, dental, housing, food, transportation, behavioral health, or another service.
How can a person contact the provider? Telephone, website, email, or another available method.
A provider is the organization, business, agency, or other entity that provides the service.
A ServiceChannel describes a way a person can access or receive a service. Examples include a website, telephone service, or physical location.
1.3 Decision Information
Decision Information explains why a person may choose one resource instead of another for a particular need.
Information can include:
Why the resource is a good match.
How closely the service matches the person's need.
How urgent the need is.
What the person must do to get started.
Whether a referral is required.
What eligibility requirements must be met.
Whether the service is currently available.
Other information that could affect the person's decision.
Decision Information can be contextual. Information such as selectionReason, match, ranking, urgency, and alternativeResources may depend on a particular person's need, search, or comparison set rather than being permanent characteristics of the resource.
Some decision information does not have a specific Schema.org property.
When Schema.org does not provide a specific property for information the project needs to record, additionalProperty and PropertyValue provide a structured way to include that information.
Purpose of the Three-Part Structure
The three-part structure provides the minimum information necessary for a person, Happi.mni, an AI system, or a search engine to determine whether a resource is:
Relevant — Accessible — Appropriate — Affordable — Worth pursuing.
Technical Implementation and Mapping
How the Resource Information Is Mapped to Schema.org
The technical process begins with the information a person needs to know before deciding whether a resource is useful.
The project then identifies the Schema.org type or property that best represents each piece of required resource information.
The selected Schema.org types and properties become the fields used to describe the resource.
2.1 Determine What a Person Needs to Know
For each resource, determine what information a person needs to decide whether the resource is:
Relevant.
Accessible.
Appropriate.
Affordable.
Worth pursuing.
The required information may include basic resource information, services offered, eligibility, access methods, hours, location, intake procedures, referral requirements, availability, and other decision information.
2.2 Find the Corresponding Schema.org Property
For each required piece of information, find the corresponding Schema.org property or type when one exists.
For example:
Resource name → name
Website → url
Telephone number → telephone
Address → address
Service area → areaServed
Population served → audience
Service access → availableChannel
Service hours → hoursAvailable
The project uses the Schema.org property that most appropriately represents the information being recorded.
2.3 Add the Schema.org Properties as Fields
The selected Schema.org properties become fields in the resource listing.
The resource listing therefore contains fields based on what a person needs to know, rather than starting with a predetermined list of business-directory fields.
When Schema.org provides an appropriate property, the project uses that property.
When the project needs information that does not have an appropriate Schema.org property, the project can use a structured project-specific field for that information.
2.4 Populate the Fields
The fields can be populated from different sources.
A field may be populated by:
An API.
A scraper.
A person conducting research.
Another verified source.
The source does not change what the field means. The source provides the value that belongs in the field.
2.5 Review and Validate the Resource Information
The completed resource information must be reviewed so the resource listing represents the resource accurately.
Validation can include checking:
Whether the resource still provides the listed service.
Whether the contact information is current.
Whether the location is correct.
Whether the hours are current.
Whether eligibility and access requirements are accurate.
When the information was last checked.
Where the information came from.
The resource listing can also record the source and validation information so the reliability of the recorded information can be understood.
2.6 Use the Completed Resource Listing
The completed resource listing provides the structured resource information that will ultimately be used on the webpage.
The exact method for turning the completed resource listing into the webpage has not yet been determined.
The project does not currently assume a particular backend, database, JavaScript process, or other technical implementation for generating the webpage.
Those implementation decisions can be made after the resource-information and Schema.org structure have been established.
The Process
The process can therefore be summarized as:
What does a person need to know?
→ Find the corresponding Schema.org type or property.
→ Add the type or property as a field.
→ Populate the field from an API, scraper, or person.
→ Review and validate the resource information.
→ Use the completed resource listing on the webpage.
The purpose of the technical mapping is to make sure the resource information needed by people is also represented in a structure that Happi.mni, AI systems, and search engines can understand.
The Resource Card Fields
The resource card contains the fields used to record the information needed about a resource.
Each field represents a specific piece of resource information.
The fields use Schema.org types and properties whenever an appropriate Schema.org term exists. Project-specific decision fields are included when the information is necessary for choosing or evaluating a resource.
3.1 Resource Identification
identifier — unique resource ID.
name — name of the resource.
description — description of the resource.
additionalType — specific type of resource.
serviceType — services provided.
category — specialties or service categories.
url — resource website.
3.2 Resource Contact and Location
telephone — telephone number.
address — physical address.
streetAddress — street address.
addressLocality — city.
addressRegion — state.
postalCode — ZIP code.
addressCountry — country.
contactPoint — additional contact information.
sameAs — other websites representing the same resource.
3.3 Service Information
areaServed — geographic area served.
providerMobility — whether the service is mobile or tied to a fixed location.
hoursAvailable — when the service is available.
availableChannel — how the service can be accessed.
availableLanguage — languages available through the service.
serviceLocation — location where the service is provided.
maximumAttendeeCapacity — maximum number of people the service location can accommodate when applicable.
geo — geographic coordinates.
audience — population served.
serviceOutput — what the person receives from the service.
3.4 Services Offered
hasOfferCatalog identifies the services offered by the resource.
Each service can include:
name — name of the service.
description — description of the service.
This allows one resource card to identify multiple services offered by the same resource.
3.5 Accessibility
amenityFeature identifies important features of the resource location.
Examples include:
Wheelchair-accessible entrance.
Other accessibility features relevant to using the resource.
3.6 Decision Information
The resource card also contains information that helps a person determine whether the resource is a good choice for the person's need.
These fields include:
selectionReason — why the resource was selected as a possible match.
eligibilityRequirements — requirements a person must meet.
availability — current availability when known.
match — how closely the resource matches the person's need.
ranking — the resource's position among other possible resources in a particular search or comparison context.
intake — what a person needs to do to begin receiving the service.
urgency — how quickly the service may be needed.
referralRequired — whether a referral is required.
goodToKnow — other information that could affect the person's decision.
alternativeResources — other resources that may meet the same need in a particular search or decision context.
Decision Information may change depending on the person's need, search, or comparison set. It does not necessarily represent a permanent characteristic of the resource.
3.7 Resource Information Quality
The resource card also records information about the source and reliability of the resource information.
These fields include:
source — where the resource information came from.
sourceType — type of source used.
validationDate — date the resource information was checked.
confidence — confidence in the accuracy of the recorded resource information.
3.8 Resource Relationships
The resource card can identify relationships with other resources.
isSimilarTo — resources that provide similar services.
isRelatedTo — resources that are related to the resource.
aggregateRating — available rating information.
Purpose of the Fields
Together, the fields provide the information needed to understand, access, compare, and choose a resource.
The resource card is therefore designed to answer the questions that matter to the person using the directory:
Who? What? Who is it for? Where? When? How? What does the person need to do? And why might this resource be the right choice?
The Standard for a Living Resource
A resource listing must never settle for being a fixed collection of facts.
The person using the directory comes first.
When a person asks a question about a resource, the question tells us what that person needs to know. If the resource listing does not answer the question, the listing is incomplete.
We do not blame the person for asking.
We do not tell the person to search somewhere else when we can find the answer.
We do not accept missing information simply because other directories leave the same information out.
We find the answer. We verify the answer. We add the answer.
That is the standard.
4.1 Listen to the Person
A person's question matters.
Questions such as these identify information a person needs before deciding whether to pursue a resource:
Do I need an appointment?
Can I walk in?
Do I need a referral? If so, who can make the referral and where do I get the referral?
What documents do I need?
Is the service available now?
Does this resource serve my particular situation?
Does this resource serve my location?
What do I need to do first?
Every question deserves an answer when the answer can be found.
4.2 Treat Missing Information as a Requirement
When a person asks a question that the resource listing cannot answer, the missing information becomes a required need for the resource listing.
We do not call the missing information optional simply because the original listing did not include it.
We do not wait for another person to ask the same question.
One person's unanswered question can become the information that helps everyone who comes after that person.
4.3 Find and Verify the Answer
We find the answer using the best available source.
Sources may include:
The resource itself.
An official government source.
An API.
A reliable scraper.
Person-conducted research.
Other appropriate sources that provide verifiable information.
We verify the answer before we add it to the resource listing.
We do not turn guesses into resource information.
4.4 Improve the Resource Listing
We add the verified answer to the resource listing so the listing meets the need identified by the person's question.
When Schema.org provides an appropriate property, we use that Schema.org property.
When Schema.org does not provide an appropriate property, we create a structured project-specific field for the information.
The resource listing changes because a person needed it to change.
4.5 Make the Answer Available to the Next Person
Once we add and verify the answer, the next person can find the answer without having to ask the same question.
Happi.mni can use the answer when helping a person evaluate the resource.
AI systems can use the structured resource information to understand the resource and its services.
Search engines can use the information to make stronger connections between searches and resources.
A question from one person can therefore improve the experience for thousands of people who come later.
The Living Resource
A living resource follows this behavior:
Person asks a question → We identify the missing information → We find the answer → We verify the answer → We add the answer → The next person benefits.
The directory must continue this cycle.
We do not measure success by how many resources we list.
We measure success by whether a person can find what they need to know to make a good decision about using a resource.
The Standard
When we discover that a person needs information, we have a responsibility to pursue that information.
Leaving known questions unanswered means knowingly leaving the next person with the same problem.
Doing less is not the standard.
The person using the directory is the most important person in the directory.
Every resource listing, every field, every answer, and every improvement exists to serve that person.
We will live up to that standard.
Reference
